{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube root cube root","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","fileName":"Cube Root.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube Root","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Exponents and Roots]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube","Square Root","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube root cube root","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","fileName":"Cube Root.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube Root","heading":"Cube Root","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A cube root of (a) is a number whose [[Cube]] equals (a):\n\n$$b=\\sqrt[3]{a}\\quad\\text{when}\\quad b^3=a.$$\n\nFor example, (sqrt[3]{27}=3) and (sqrt[3]{-27}=-3). Because cubing preserves sign and is one-to-one on real numbers, every real number has exactly one real cube root. The index (3) is written on the radical symbol, unlike the usually omitted index (2) of a [[Square Root]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube","Square Root","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"cube root cube root","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Cube Root.md","fileName":"Cube Root.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Cube Root","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algebraessentials.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Exponents and Roots","Cube","Square Root","algebraessen